Hi Mathias, Michal,

Thank you for the analysis and the requests for additional data.

I made a final set of tests on the Fedora 44 kernel containing both
commits:

7c0c31c66a7f9daace156bac427aafb2f4bbb5fc
3d9eeb336131bc5a174367c384fa00c15c8744fd

The running kernel was:

7.1.8-200.xhci3d9eeb3.fc44.x86_64

IOMMU remained enabled in translated, strict mode:

intel_iommu=on iommu.passthrough=0 iommu.strict=1

No DMAR or IOMMU fault was observed.

## Behavior without debugging

At 640x480 YUYV and a requested 30 fps, with
exposure_dynamic_framerate=1, a normal 120-frame run timed out after
45.034 seconds with rc=124.

With exposure_dynamic_framerate=0, the camera is no longer completely
stuck, but the video is still not usable as a smooth stream:

* One 120-frame run completed in 15.109 seconds, giving an effective
   7.94 delivered fps and 304 skipped sequence numbers.
* Five consecutive restart runs all completed with rc=0, but their
   effective delivered rates ranged from 3.71 to 21.08 fps.
* Across those five runs, 600 buffers were delivered and 1477 sequence
   gaps were reported over 75.143 seconds.

The visible result is a working picture with frequent and sometimes
long freezes. Therefore fixing the "All TDs skipped" condition restored
ring synchronization, but did not make the camera practically usable
at its default setting.

## UVC alternate setting

With uvcvideo trace=0x400, both values of
exposure_dynamic_framerate selected the same streaming configuration:

Device requested 3072 B/frame bandwidth
Selecting alternate setting 7 (3072 B/frame bandwidth)
Allocated 5 URB buffers of 32x3072 bytes each

Thus changing exposure_dynamic_framerate does not change the USB
alternate setting in the 640x480 tests.

## xHCI tracepoint captures

I captured two complete eight-second xHCI tracepoint windows with room
lighting on. xHCI dynamic debug was disabled. Both streams were
deliberately terminated after eight seconds, so rc=124 in these two
trace result files only denotes the intentional timeout.

For exposure_dynamic_framerate=1:

* interval: 8.048177 seconds
* trace entries: 198668/198668, with no trace-buffer loss
* raw trace size: 40,648,949 bytes
* completed buffers printed by v4l2-ctl: 0
* camera isochronous URBs enqueued/given back: 1972/1972
* camera isochronous TRBs queued: 65076
* Missed Service Errors for slot 4 endpoint ID 3: 4260

For exposure_dynamic_framerate=0:

* interval: 8.052311 seconds
* trace entries: 201441/201441, with no trace-buffer loss
* raw trace size: 40,942,997 bytes
* completed buffers printed by v4l2-ctl: 51
* sequence gaps reported by v4l2-ctl: 167
* camera isochronous URBs enqueued/given back: 1973/1973
* camera isochronous TRBs queued: 65109
* Missed Service Errors for slot 4 endpoint ID 3: 1514

Disabling dynamic frame rate therefore reduced the number of Missed
Service Errors by about 64.5%, but did not eliminate them or the visible
frame loss.

Neither exact trace window contains "All TDs skipped", "Event dma ...
not part of TD", or "no TDs queued". This seems consistent with commit
3d9eeb336131 fixing the previous ring-desynchronization condition.

## TRB layout

Each 98304-byte camera URB consists of 32 isochronous packets of 3072
bytes. The trace contains 33 TRBs per URB because one 3072-byte packet
crosses a 64 KiB boundary and is represented by a multi-TRB TD:

* Isoch TRB: length 1024, TD size 2, TBC 0, TLBPC 2
* Normal TRB: length 2048, TD size 0

The remaining 31 packets use one 3072-byte Isoch TRB each, with TBC 0
and TLBPC 2. The layout is the same with
exposure_dynamic_framerate=1 and 0.

The README in the Linux attachment also contains a breakdown of which
TRBs the Missed Service Error event pointers mapped to. The attachment
contains both complete raw trace files.

The status interrupt endpoint behavior is now also clearer. Each trace
contains a burst of 21 cancel/set-dequeue operations for USB endpoint
0x83 during stream setup. The bursts last only about 1.77 ms, followed
by one final cancellation during teardown roughly eight seconds later.
There is no 15-second cancellation interval in these captures. This
supports the interpretation that the status endpoint activity is not
the cause of the continuing video loss.

## Requested registers

The requested register values are:

HCCPARAMS2 = 0x0000007d
USBCMD     = 0x00000005

Complete reg-cap and reg-op dumps are included in the Linux attachment.

## Lower-resolution test

I also tried 320x240 YUYV at a requested 30 fps with
exposure_dynamic_framerate=0 and no debugging.

The run completed with rc=0 and delivered all 300 requested buffers,
but it took approximately 50 seconds and reported 1171 skipped sequence
numbers. That is an effective delivered rate of about 6.0 fps, with
only 20.39% of sequence positions delivered.

Therefore reducing the resolution did not improve the practical result
in this run.

Unfortunately, the trace=0x400 journal capture attempted for this mode
returned no entries. I therefore do not know which alternate setting
or packet size was selected at 320x240, and cannot claim that this was
a power-of-two packet-size test. The raw user-space result is included
in the Linux attachment.

## Windows 10 comparison

I also tested the same built-in camera on the same laptop under Windows
10 Pro 22H2, using 640x480 YUY2 at a requested 30 fps, automatic
exposure and constant bright external lighting.

With Windows Low Light Compensation enabled:

* 300 frames took 43.185 seconds wall-clock
* effective delivered rate: 6.95 fps

With Low Light Compensation disabled:

* FFmpeg captured 300 frames in 10.04 seconds
* FFmpeg stream rate: 29.88 fps
* total process time including startup and shutdown: 11.446 seconds

I then performed 40 consecutive stream restarts with Low Light
Compensation disabled. Every individual FFmpeg log reached its final
120-frame record. The streams took approximately 3.99 to 4.06 seconds
each at 30 fps; total process durations including startup and shutdown
were 5.380 to 5.554 seconds. No 15-second stall occurred.

A short Windows USB ETW capture from a successful 120-frame run is also
included. I understand that it does not expose xHCI transfer-ring state
or TRB fields at the same level as the Linux tracepoints, so I provide
it mainly as a behavioral comparison.

This makes the comparison quite pronounced: disabling the corresponding
low-light/dynamic-frame-rate behavior gives Windows a stable 30 fps
stream and reliable restarts, while under Linux it only partially
reduces Missed Service Errors and leaves severe frame loss.

